From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: crypto: ecc - Fix P-521 private keys, key generation and ECDH

ecc_gen_privkey() takes ndigits * 8 = 72 random bytes for P-521, a
576-bit number, which is almost never below the 521-bit order: key
generation fails. Keep the order's bit length of the random bits, as
FIPS 186-5 A.2.2 asks.

ecc_is_key_valid() wants the key to be ndigits * 8 bytes long; for P-521
also take the curve size, 66 bytes.

crypto_ecdh_shared_secret() starts the ladder from a random Z of
ndigits * 8 bytes. For P-521 that is up to 576 bits, but
vli_mmod_fast_521() only reduces products of numbers below 2^521: with
the extra bits the shared secret comes out wrong. Keep Z to the curve's
bit length too.

ecc_point_mult() makes the scalar k + n or k + 2n so that the ladder can
start from a fixed top bit. For P-521, k + n never carries out of the
nine digits, so it always took k + 2n with bit 522 as the top bit, which
is set only for k above about 2^260: smaller keys (ECDH accepts keys
from 2) gave (k + 2^522) * P. Pick whichever of k + n and k + 2n has bit
521 set and start there. Mainline only calls it with k = n on P-521.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---
 crypto/ecc.c | 20 +++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/crypto/ecc.c b/crypto/ecc.c
index c7366eb..67dcea3 100644
--- a/crypto/ecc.c
+++ b/crypto/ecc.c
@@ -1365,11 +1365,14 @@ static void ecc_point_mult(struct ecc_point *result,
 
 	carry = vli_add(sk[0], scalar, curve->n, ndigits);
 	vli_add(sk[1], sk[0], curve->n, ndigits);
-	scalar = sk[!carry];
-	if (curve->nbits == 521)	/* NIST P521 */
-		num_bits = curve->nbits + 2;
-	else
+	if (curve->nbits == 521) {	/* NIST P521 */
+		/* k + n or k + 2n, whichever has bit 521 as its top bit */
+		carry = vli_test_bit(sk[0], curve->nbits);
+		num_bits = curve->nbits + 1;
+	} else {
 		num_bits = sizeof(u64) * ndigits * 8 + 1;
+	}
+	scalar = sk[!carry];
 
 	vli_set(rx[1], point->x, ndigits);
 	vli_set(ry[1], point->y, ndigits);
@@ -1556,7 +1559,9 @@ int ecc_is_key_valid(unsigned int curve_id, unsigned int ndigits,
 
 	nbytes = ndigits << ECC_DIGITS_TO_BYTES_SHIFT;
 
-	if (private_key_len != nbytes)
+	/* P-521: 66 or 72 bytes */
+	if (private_key_len != nbytes &&
+	    private_key_len != DIV_ROUND_UP(curve->nbits, 8))
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	return __ecc_is_key_valid(curve, private_key, ndigits);
@@ -1600,6 +1605,8 @@ int ecc_gen_privkey(unsigned int curve_id, unsigned int ndigits,
 	err = crypto_stdrng_get_bytes(private_key, nbytes);
 	if (err)
 		return err;
+	if (nbits % 64)
+		private_key[ndigits - 1] &= GENMASK_ULL(nbits % 64 - 1, 0);
 
 	/* Step 4: make sure the private key is in the valid range. */
 	if (__ecc_is_key_valid(curve, private_key, ndigits))
@@ -1723,6 +1730,9 @@ int crypto_ecdh_shared_secret(unsigned int curve_id, unsigned int ndigits,
 	nbytes = ndigits << ECC_DIGITS_TO_BYTES_SHIFT;
 
 	get_random_bytes(rand_z, nbytes);
+	/* P-521: the fast reduction only takes products of 521-bit numbers */
+	if (curve->nbits % 64)
+		rand_z[ndigits - 1] &= GENMASK_ULL(curve->nbits % 64 - 1, 0);
 
 	pk = ecc_alloc_point(ndigits);
 	if (!pk) {
